  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nA dynamic system continues impacting the forecast area. Currently, \nsurface low is located over eastern Montana/southwestern North \nDakota, while upper low is primarily over north central Montana \npropagating into south central Canada. For the most part,\nthunderstorms from earlier today have exited the CWA. However, \nanother severe weather threat persists this afternoon, and seems\nespecially focused along and west of Highway 83 where skies \ncleared out quicker in the day. RAP analysis suggests a narrow \ncorridor of enhanced instability this afternoon that will be \ncollocated with 40 to 50 kts of deep layer shear. Wherever this \nultimately sets up would be the primary focus for severe \nweather. With bulk shear vectors generally perpendicular to the\nfrontal boundary off the surface low, supercells are possible \nthat could lead to large hail and damaging winds. A tornado or \ntwo cannot be ruled out, especially near the surface low. \n\nBy this evening, any remaining instability should rapidly decrease \nas dry air moves in, while CIN increases. Thus any severe weather \nthreat should also diminish as the sun sets. After which, a few \nshowers are possible mainly in the north on Tuesday, with additional \nrain chances overnight. Gusty westerly winds will develop Tuesday\nas the surface low lifts northeast, especially in the northern \nhalf of the state. Though pressure rises and CAA advection \naren't the strongest, there was enough confidence to issue a \nWind Advisory for most counties north of I94. \n\nFor Tuesday, the strongest winds are not collocated with the \ndriest air. Still, critical fire weather conditions are \npossible in the far southwest. In addition, near critical \nconditions are expected in the remainder of the southwest and \nsouth central. Drier conditions and breezy winds could once \nagain produce near critical to critical fire weather conditions \nacross much of the southwest and south central on Thursday.\n\nBeyond Tuesday, mostly dry conditions are favored through the\nremainder of the workweek. After which, widespread \nprecipitation chances return Sunday and into early next week.\nTemperatures Tuesday and Wednesday will be seasonable before\nanother brief warm-up Thursday. A cooling trend is then expected\nthis weekend and into early next week with highs possibly in \nthe upper 50s and 60s by early next week.",
